                        "language": "english",
                        "review": "Believe it or not, this review is toned down and more positive than my original draft! I really wanted to love Meganoid; I just can't. Here's why!\n\n[h1]Expectations[/h1]\n\nI bought Meganoid on a whim after watching the trailer and thinking : Oh cool, hacking turrets and traps in a rogue-like platformer? I'm in!\n\nI imagined a game similar to Spelunky but with a hacking twist and a sci-fi flavor.\n\n[h1]Hacking[/h1]\n\n\"Hacking\" involves spending a green keycard to \"hack\" a random system in-between levels. Cashing in a collectible for random effects is not hacking. I was expecting a level of interactivity on par with Zillion (a game that is 30 years old!), maybe a shade of Gunpoint...nope! Hacking here is just a label, like in the first Watchdogs.\n\n[h1]Controls[/h1]\n\nThe controls are serviceable enough yet unintuitive.\n\nYour character can hang onto ledges in all situations....except when there is a full wall beneath the ledge and a keycard-operated machine next to the wall. Bombing the wall will change something in the game which re-enables the character to latch onto the ledge. It might sound trivial but this exact situation showed up in 90% of my playthroughs, which lead us to...\n\n[h1]Procedural Generation[/h1]\n\nProcedural generation is one of those things that is hard to get right. While it mostly works here, it creates uninteresting and instantly-predictable levels, sometimes putting every possible thing in a single level. A bit of refinement in this area would help make the game interesting. The Meganoid is supposed to be a spaceship yet the levels fail to convey that fact; You might as well be in a mine, space station, factory, etc.\n\n[h1]Audio[/h1]\n\nThe sounds are neither cohesive nor memorable; the lack of music might be excuseable if the ambient sounds would flesh out the world but again, this important aspect fails to convey the atmosphere one might expect from a gigantic spaceship.\n\n[h1]Graphics[/h1]\n\nThe pixel art has no identity, none of it memorable, most of it messy and hard to read.\n\nOf special mention is the underwater waving effect, which runs at a ridiculously high frequency and feels more like a tech demo instead of conveying the sense of being underwater. Game Boy Advance games (Metroid Fusion, for instance) manage to pull it off much more effectively; it's all about attention to detail and cohesiveness.\n\n[h1]Conclusion[/h1]\n\nEven though I was facing disappointment at every turn, I kept giving chances to the game...until I eventually gave up.\nThe game constantly reminds me of better games, making Meganoid feel derivative at best.\nThe game is simply not engaging and it feels unfinished.",
